   "problem": "A flexible arm driven by a DC motor keeps vibrating at the tip after every move. Our target was to cut the settling time of that vibration by at least 30% with a PID controller, starting from measured data, not a datasheet. The arm is very lightly damped, so left alone the vibration takes a long time to die out. Any controller also had to keep the first peak low, because a big swing at the tip is the same problem in a different form.",
   "options": {
    "head": [
     "Controller",
     "What it showed",
     "Kept?"
    ],
    "rows": [
     [
      "Open loop",
      "Baseline response of the lightly damped arm",
      "Baseline only"
     ],
     [
      "Unity feedback",
      "First closed-loop comparison",
      "No"
     ],
     [
      "Proportional only",
      "Effect of gain alone",
      "No"
     ],
     [
      "PID, higher gain sets",
      "Faster settling, but excessive overshoot",
      "No"
     ],
     [
      "PID, Kp 500, Ki 320, Kd 180",
      "Fast settling with an acceptable peak",
      "Yes"
     ]
    ]
   },
   "process": [
    {
     "text": "**Identify.** Used the logarithmic decrement on the measured tip vibration from an accelerometer. Damping ratio came out at 0.0047 and natural frequency at 47.75 rad/s."
    },
    {
     "text": "**Model.** Built the second order model G(s) = 2280 / (s² + 0.4477s + 2280) in MATLAB."
    },
    {
     "text": "**Compare.** Ran open loop, unity feedback and proportional-only responses as baselines."
    },
    {
     "text": "**Tune.** Ran 5 PID gain sets and tracked rise time, settling time, overshoot and peak for each. The higher-gain trials, with Kd up to 800, settled faster but overshot too far."
    },
    {
     "text": "**Choose.** Picked the set with the best balance between settling time and peak, not the fastest one."
    }
   ],
   "result": "Kp 500, Ki 320, Kd 180 gave a stable, well-damped tip response and met the settling time target. We chose it over a faster-settling set because that set had a higher peak, and a high peak on a flexible arm is the problem we set out to fix.",
   "reflection": "Our whole model came from one measured decay, so every gain we tuned leaned on that single record. If I did it again, I would take several decay records, check that the damping ratio holds across them, and then test the chosen gains on the real arm instead of only in simulation.",
